detail.abstract:
Traditional tactical asset allocation models typically rely on binary regime filters that classify markets as either bullish or bearish. While widely utilized for drawdown management, these approaches often generate excessive turnover and whipsaw trades during periods of market uncertainty when technical signals conflict. This study introduces the Ternary Regime Allocation Framework, TRAF: a structural extension to binary regime models that explicitly recognizes a third, transitional market state characterized by disagreement between trend and momentum signals.&nbsp;
<div>
 Using the Elder Impulse System, a widely available technical framework that can be readily replicated across numerous online charting platforms, TRAF defines three distinct regimes: Green (bullish), Red (bearish), and Blue (signal disagreement). During Blue periods, TRAF applies a state-persistence or sticky rule in which the portfolio maintains its prior allocation rather than executing an immediate regime change. The approach is evaluated against a passive S&amp;P 500 benchmark and a conventional binary trend-following model using monthly data from 1977 through 2026; defensive allocation is modeled using a monthly total return approximation of 3-month U.S. Treasury bills (FRED: TB3MS).&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The results demonstrate that TRAF materially improves risk-adjusted performance while preserving the long-term return characteristics of the equity risk premium. The strategy achieves a compound annual growth rate of 11.21 percent, comparable to the passive benchmark at 11.42 percent, while reducing annualized volatility from 15.12 percent to 9.84 percent. The Ulcer Index declines from 12.45 to 2.84, representing a 77 percent improvement in drawdown stability. In addition, the TRAF structure reduces one-way turnover by more than 40 percent relative to binary models, suggesting meaningful transaction cost and tax advantages.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Beyond these quantitative improvements, TRAF provides a practical behavioral advantage: during periods of signal conflict, it replaces discretionary decision-making with a systematic rule for maintaining prior exposure. This creates a psychological color ease for the practitioner, effectively formalizing the mandate for patience. Robustness analysis demonstrates consistent performance across a range of exponential moving average parameters, between 11 and 15 months, and across multiple macroeconomic sub-periods. These findings suggest that market disagreement represents a distinct and observable regime; by explicitly incorporating this state, TRAF improves both portfolio outcomes and investor adherence across diverse market environments.
</div>
